      "text": "<text>[asy]\nsize(150);\ndot((0,0)); label(\"Let a point (alpha, beta) on the coordinate plane coincide with the coordinates of Point A below.\", (6,4));\nlabel(\"Point A\");\ndot((6,4));\nlabel(\"Point B\");\ndot((6,-16));\nlabel(\"If you connect point B with point A, the line which we just drew is what is called a diagonal (برzier line if you will) of the original coordinate plane. \");\nlabel(\"$\\alpha$\", (6,4));\nlabel(\"$\\beta$\", (6,-16));\n[/asy] $\\alpha$ is inversely proportional to $\\beta$, meaning our equation will look like: \\[\\alpha = \\frac{k}{\\beta},\\]where $k$ is some constant. We can let $x_1 = 4$ when $y_1 = 9$, so now we can create our system of equations: \\[4 = \\frac{k}{9},\\]and, \\[y = \\frac{k}{x}.\\]We can then solve for k in both equations and set them equal so that we can solve for x. \\[\\frac{k}{9} = 4,\\]and \\[\\frac{k}{x} = \\frac{k}{4},\\]yielding of course that $x = \\boxed{-72}$. The answer is -72",
